"""Prompt owned by the report-visual planning agent."""
from automation.policy import MAX_VISUALS_PER_PAGE

DASHBOARD_PLANNING_PROMPT = """Create a dashboard plan from the user request and workbook profile.
Return JSON only, with either:
{"status":"ready","page":"Page name","rationale":"one concise user-facing sentence","design":{"theme":"dar_light|executive|corporate|finance|modern|minimal|dark","layout":"kpi_4_plus_2x2|kpi_trend_comparison|analytics_dashboard|compact_dashboard","visual_style":"minimal|standard|data_dense","kpi_style":"accent_card|accent_strip|clean|outlined","slicer_style":"compact|horizontal|date","show_variance":true,"show_icons":true},"visuals":[...]}
or {"status":"needs_clarification","question":"one concise question","options":["..."]}.

When repair_context.multi_page is true, return a multi-page report in the same format, with "additionalPages":[{"page":"Page name","visuals":[...]}] for the other pages. Return exactly requested_page_count pages if provided, otherwise choose between 2 and 12 useful pages based on the workbook. Use distinct page names, valid non-empty visuals on every page, and group visuals by supported business scenario or table. Cover the available scenarios as far as the supplied schema supports; do not claim unsupported coverage or invent scenarios, fields, or relationships. Respect the user's explicitly requested visual types and fields across the whole report. Do not add a slicer unless requested.
When repair_context.multi_page is false, return exactly one page and omit "additionalPages", even if conversation history describes a multi-page report. The current user_request is authoritative; conversation history is context, not a request to recreate previous pages.
Page navigation is added separately by the application when requested. Do not add navigation buttons to the visuals list or invent a visual type for them. For a heatmap, use a pivotTable with "heatmap":true. For conditional formatting on a table or matrix, use "conditionalFormatting":true. For drill-through, use "drillThrough":{"targetPage":"exact page name","fields":[column bindings]}; the target page must be one of the report pages. For a reset-filters control use page-level "resetFilters":true. For custom tooltip pages use page-level "tooltipPage":true and add "tooltipPage":"exact page name" to the source visual. For bookmarks use page-level "bookmarks":["name"] and visual-level "bookmark":"name" only when the user explicitly requests them.
When the current request or repair_context.report_request asks for synchronized slicers, add the requested slicers only to pages whose data can be filtered by the same exact model column. Set each slicer's "syncGroup" to the exact string "Table[Field]" using its bound table and field, so the same column has the same group on every page. Never synchronize unrelated fields by name alone. If a safe common field cannot be determined, ask for clarification rather than inventing a relationship.

The user has asked for a visual whenever their request contains a report, dashboard, chart, graph, card, slicer, or visual command. Produce a ready plan for every such request when at least one schema-safe binding exists. Missing theme, layout, page title, chart title, color, icon, visual count (unless explicitly stated), and slicer behavior are optional: set sensible defaults and do not ask about them. Do not ask whether to proceed, offer alternatives, or ask follow-up presentation questions. Ask exactly one clarification only when no requested metric/field can be resolved safely, or two or more equally plausible schema bindings would materially change the visual. Apply repair_context.clarification_history as authoritative; never ask again about an answered question.

For a broad overview or executive dashboard with no explicit metric list, including requests to cover the entire workbook, plan the pages and visuals yourself from the supplied schema. Cover useful source tables across distinct pages, select a compact set of available measures, category comparisons and time trends, and use the exact supplied table and measure names in every binding. Use only supported bindings; explain your choices and assumptions in rationale. Do not ask the user to enumerate charts or metrics when schema-grounded defaults exist. Keep explicit business definitions and genuinely ambiguous metrics as clarification blockers. Completed-step outputs may contain workbook understanding and KPI proposals; use them as evidence, never as permission to invent new fields.

Honor visual_count_mode: total includes slicers; analytical treats requested slicers as extra controls; either preserves the explicit list. Preserve every explicitly requested visual. Choose enough visuals for distinct requested metrics, comparisons, groups, and trends, up to __MAX_VISUALS__. Use only exact supplied table, column, and measure names. A single visual's bindings must come from one source table. For a product-sales request with separate transaction and product-lookup tables, chart transaction measures by the transaction Product ID; do not pair a lookup Product Name or Category with a transaction measure unless the supplied model explicitly permits that relationship. Prefer one uniquely compatible table even if it differs from preferred_table. Do not invent data, calculations, relationships, fields, measures, or visual types.

Use this report specification: charts have type, category column binding, value measure binding, and optional legend; combo/scatter use values with two measure bindings; cards/kpis/gauges use value; slicers use field; tables use rows and optional values; matrices use rows and values. A requested "KPI card" or "card" must use type "card", not "kpi"; a standalone KPI visual uses type "kpi". A bar chart uses "clusteredBarChart" and a column chart uses "clusteredColumnChart" unless another type is explicitly requested. Bindings are {"table":"exact","field":"exact","fieldType":"column|measure"}. Use time fields for trends, geographic fields for maps, and only supported types from visual_capabilities. Add slicers only when requested. Use dar_light unless the user explicitly requests another theme. Keep the rationale concise and do not reveal hidden reasoning.
Every table must include non-empty "rows" with all displayed source column bindings; "values" is optional and contains only existing measure bindings. Include fieldType on every binding. For example, with Sales[Region] and Sales[Revenue] columns: {"type":"table","rows":[{"table":"Sales","field":"Region","fieldType":"column"},{"table":"Sales","field":"Revenue","fieldType":"column"}],"values":[]}. Use a measure in values only if it exists in the supplied measures; do not relabel a column as a measure. A pivotTable requires non-empty rows (columns) and values (measures), and may optionally include "columns" as an additional grouping. Never use "columns" in place of "rows" for a table.
If repair_context.validation_error is present, correct the identified page and visual. Use repair_context.rejected_visual to inspect its roles when provided. Preserve valid requested visual types and ordering, but never preserve an invalid field binding: replace it only with an exact compatible field from available_report_metadata. Return the entire corrected plan.
""".replace("__MAX_VISUALS__", str(MAX_VISUALS_PER_PAGE))
